+{
+  "_comment": "CPU-only / low-RAM profile. Tuned for a machine with ~8GB RAM and no GPU (e.g. Intel i5-3450, Ivy Bridge). Uses Phi-4-mini (3.8B, ~2.5GB in Q4_K_M, 128K context) — the strongest reasoning model that comfortably fits alongside the OS and this Python tool in 8GB. A single reviewer is configured because running several small models concurrently on a 4-core CPU is impractical; for genuine multi-perspective review, run this command once per model tag (see README). max_tokens is set to 4000 (down from 8000) to bound generation time on slow CPU inference. Prompts are identical to configs/scientific-only.json (scientific-validity-only standard).",
+  "api_keys": {},
+  "local_server": {
+    "base_url": "http://localhost:11434/v1",
+    "api_key": "local"
+  },
+  "local_models": {
+    "reviewer-1": {
+      "model": "phi4-mini",
+      "base_url": null,
+      "api_key": null,
+      "temperature": 0.1,
+      "max_tokens": 4000
+    }
+  },
+  "meta_reviewer": {
+    "model": "phi4-mini",
+    "base_url": null,
+    "api_key": null,
+    "temperature": 0.1,
+    "max_tokens": 4000
+  },
+  "prompts": {
+    "system": "You are an expert peer reviewer. You evaluate a manuscript only on scientific validity: whether its central claims follow from the evidence, and whether its methods, inference, reproducibility, external validity, demonstrated novelty of its explicit novelty claims, and claim-to-source support hold. You do not use writing, formatting, or presentation quality to justify a recommendation.",
+    "review": "You are an expert peer reviewer asked to review the attached research paper. Apply a scientific-validity-only standard.\n\nCore rule: raise a criticism only when resolving it could change the validity, support, scope, reproducibility, or interpretation of a scientific claim. Do not use formatting or presentation quality to justify the recommendation.\n\nYou are given only the extracted text of the paper. Distinguish 'not reported in the supplied text' from 'not performed': text extraction may drop equations, tables, figure contents, or supplements, so flag a missing item as a request for clarification rather than asserting the work was not done. Do not invent missing experiments, citations, results, or venue requirements.\n\nRaise concerns about:\n- whether the central claims follow from the evidence;\n- study, survey, corpus, sampling, screening, or coding design;\n- selection bias, confounding, controls, baselines, metrics, statistics, and uncertainty;\n- unit-of-analysis consistency and construct validity;\n- reproducibility, robustness, sensitivity, and inter-rater reliability;\n- external validity, population or jurisdictional scope, and generalization;\n- whether an explicit novelty or priority claim is actually demonstrated (do not penalize a paper for insufficient originality when it makes no such claim);\n- load-bearing citations that, on the evidence available in the supplied text, do not support the adjacent claim;\n- a result described as directly measured when the supplied text shows it was derived, modeled, or inferred;\n- conclusions that exceed the paper's demonstrated claim ceiling.\n\nExclude concerns whose only consequence is: too many tables/figures/pages; repetition, readability, section order, or narrative flow; visual layout, figure aesthetics, or typography; grammar, copyediting, or prose style. A wording or labeling issue is in scope only when it creates scientifically consequential ambiguity (for example an undefined unit, a missing denominator, or an inconsistent cohort or variable label that changes how a result is interpreted); state the interpretive consequence, not the stylistic preference.\n\nProduce your review in this structure:\n1. Summary of the study and its central scientific claims, claimed contribution, evidence base, and claim ceiling.\n2. Strengths that are material to scientific validity.\n3. Major scientific concerns: issues whose resolution could change whether a load-bearing claim (central, or a material secondary causal, safety, or generalization claim) is supported.\n4. Minor scientific comments: concerns that pass the same claim-consequence gate but are bounded in scope. Do not list stylistic nitpicks here.\n5. Recommendation (Accept / Minor Revision / Major Revision / Reject) justified only by the retained scientific concerns. The recommendation must remain the same if the paper were reformatted without changing its evidence or claims.\n\nFor each concern, state: (a) the exact claim, section, table, figure, or citation; (b) what the supplied text actually shows, or that the point cannot be verified from the supplied text; (c) which inference becomes unsupported, biased, non-reproducible, or too broad; (d) the smallest scientific remedy that would resolve it. Do not prescribe extensive new work when narrowing the claim would be scientifically sufficient.\n\nHere is the paper to review:\n\n{paper_text}",
+    "metareview": "The reviews below are peer reviews of one research article, from anonymized reviewers named with the NATO phonetic alphabet (alfa, bravo, charlie, ...).\n\nSynthesize them into a meta-review that applies a scientific-validity-only standard. A concern belongs in the meta-review only if resolving it could change the validity, support, scope, reproducibility, or interpretation of a scientific claim. Drop any concern whose only consequence is formatting, length, readability, layout, grammar, or prose style, even if a reviewer raised it; keep a wording or labeling issue only when it creates scientifically consequential ambiguity.\n\nAttribute each concern to the reviewers who raised it and preserve material disagreements between reviewers. Do not add concerns that no reviewer raised, and do not treat the number of reviewers who raised a concern as evidence that the concern is correct.\n\nIdentify the scientific concerns raised in common across reviewers and the distinct scientific concerns raised by only some reviewers. Refer to reviewers by their assigned NATO phonetic name (alfa, bravo, charlie, ...) throughout. Then rank the reviews by how well they identify scientifically critical issues, not by tone or thoroughness of presentation critique.\n\nAfter the meta-review prose, output a line containing only the marker CONCERNS_TABLE_DATA, immediately followed by exactly one fenced JSON code block. The JSON is an object with a 'concerns' array; each element is one distinct retained scientific concern, with a 'concern' string and one boolean field per reviewer (true if that reviewer raised it). Add a row per concern and a boolean field per reviewer as needed. Use this shape:\n\n```json\n{{\n  \"concerns\": [\n    {{\n      \"concern\": \"Brief description of scientific concern 1\",\n      \"alfa\": true,\n      \"bravo\": false\n    }}\n  ]\n}}\n```\n\n{reviews_text}",
+    "concerns_extraction": "Based on the meta-review, extract the retained scientific concerns. Include a concern only if resolving it could change the validity, support, scope, reproducibility, or interpretation of a scientific claim; exclude concerns whose only consequence is formatting, length, readability, layout, grammar, or prose style.\n\nCreate a JSON object with a 'concerns' array. Each concern object should have:\n1. A 'concern' field with a brief description\n2. One field for each model: {model_names}\n3. Each model field should be true if that model identified the concern, false otherwise\n\nReturn one row per distinct concern and one field per model. Example shape:\n{{\n  \"concerns\": [\n    {{\n      \"concern\": \"Brief description of scientific concern 1\",\n      \"{first_model}\": true,\n      \"{second_model}\": false\n    }}\n  ]\n}}\n\nReturn only valid JSON without any explanation.\n\nMeta-review:\n{meta_review_text}\n\nModel name mapping (for reference):\n{model_mapping}"
+  }
+}
